WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.800
Imagine you punch an address into your GPS.

00:03.160 --> 00:07.120
You hit go and you expect, you know, the fastest route to the screen.

00:07.120 --> 00:09.220
Right. Just one solid blue line.

00:09.300 --> 00:11.080
Exactly. One solid blue line.

00:11.600 --> 00:14.920
But instead of that, the screen just completely explodes.

00:15.280 --> 00:17.440
It shows you 10 million different routes.

00:17.580 --> 00:19.660
And here's the absolute kicker.

00:20.820 --> 00:27.280
Every single one of those 10 million routes takes exactly 14 minutes and 32 seconds.

00:27.500 --> 00:27.940
Wow.

00:27.940 --> 00:30.380
Yeah. They use the same amount of gas.

00:30.480 --> 00:30.720
Okay.

00:30.900 --> 00:32.680
They have the exact same number of stops.

00:32.840 --> 00:34.520
So there is no best route.

00:34.700 --> 00:38.040
They are all, mathematically speaking, perfectly correct.

00:38.140 --> 00:38.400
Right.

00:38.860 --> 00:43.720
So if correctness can no longer narrow the field down to a single outcome,

00:44.140 --> 00:46.820
how does the GPS decide which one to show you?

00:47.180 --> 00:49.140
How does the system actually choose?

00:50.280 --> 00:52.280
And I guess more importantly,

00:52.760 --> 00:56.280
when the universe itself is faced with a million perfectly correct ways

00:56.280 --> 00:59.820
for a physical event to happen, how does reality choose?

00:59.820 --> 01:04.700
I mean, it fundamentally breaks how we have traditionally thought about logic,

01:04.960 --> 01:07.880
computation, and honestly, even basic physics.

01:08.080 --> 01:08.340
Yeah.

01:08.500 --> 01:12.760
Because if correctness is no longer this ultimate filter, right,

01:12.840 --> 01:18.240
if it is no longer enough to narrow things down to a single localized outcome,

01:18.500 --> 01:23.360
then there just has to be an invisible secondary process happening behind the scenes.

01:23.360 --> 01:25.040
Something else is doing the choosing.

01:25.240 --> 01:25.800
Exactly.

01:26.060 --> 01:27.640
Something else is pulling the strings.

01:27.960 --> 01:32.300
And that invisible process, that hidden hand selecting reality,

01:32.660 --> 01:35.860
is exactly what we are exploring today.

01:36.260 --> 01:39.420
We are looking at this completely mind-bending paper

01:39.420 --> 01:42.120
by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

01:42.900 --> 01:47.240
The title is Constraint Closure and the Selection of Reality.

01:47.520 --> 01:48.420
It is quite a title.

01:48.420 --> 01:49.320
It really is.

01:49.440 --> 01:53.100
And this is not just some minor tweak to an existing algorithm.

01:53.560 --> 01:58.980
This is a massive, incredibly ambitious attempt to unify machine learning,

01:59.560 --> 02:01.680
physical field dynamics, and modern economics

02:01.680 --> 02:05.880
into a single cohesive theory of how reality comes to be.

02:06.020 --> 02:06.280
Yeah.

02:06.340 --> 02:08.680
And it argues something deeply counterintuitive.

02:08.860 --> 02:13.620
It claims that reality isn't constructed by adding things together piece by piece.

02:13.620 --> 02:19.960
It is simply what is left over when you systematically eliminate absolutely everything that doesn't fit.

02:20.260 --> 02:22.160
Which is wild to wrap your head around.

02:22.600 --> 02:23.260
It is.

02:23.500 --> 02:29.920
And our core mission here for you listening at home is to really trace that specific journey.

02:30.280 --> 02:36.620
We are going to explore this massive paradigm shift from construction to elimination.

02:37.160 --> 02:37.400
Right.

02:37.400 --> 02:43.200
We will look at how systems, whether we are talking about a trillion-parameter artificial intelligence

02:43.200 --> 02:49.020
or the microscopic laws of the physical universe or even a global financial market,

02:49.220 --> 02:53.580
how they deal with this overwhelming abundance of correct answers.

02:53.820 --> 02:54.000
Yeah.

02:54.220 --> 02:58.800
We're going to see how reality is not something built like a brick wall.

02:58.800 --> 03:08.720
It's more like reality is what remains when you eradicate every configuration that cannot globally coexist.

03:09.020 --> 03:10.860
It is a process of decimation.

03:11.060 --> 03:11.320
Okay.

03:11.460 --> 03:12.440
Let's unpack this.

03:12.800 --> 03:17.020
Because to truly understand how reality is selected in this new framework,

03:17.220 --> 03:21.560
we first have to understand why simply being correct is no longer enough.

03:21.640 --> 03:21.880
Right.

03:21.880 --> 03:29.200
And the paper points out that this realization has recently sent absolute shockwaves to the AI and machine learning communities.

03:29.660 --> 03:33.700
But let's look at the old way of thinking first, like the world we all grew up in.

03:33.740 --> 03:34.900
Yeah, the classical view.

03:35.200 --> 03:35.680
Exactly.

03:36.040 --> 03:43.220
You encounter a problem, you figure out the rules, you apply the constraints, and boom, you find the right answer.

03:43.720 --> 03:46.420
And once you have the right answer, the job is done.

03:46.420 --> 03:53.200
I mean, the classical view of reasoning operates on what we can call a selection-limited regime.

03:53.620 --> 03:54.300
Selection-limited.

03:54.620 --> 03:54.940
Right.

03:55.280 --> 04:04.180
In this classical paradigm, which encompasses traditional logic, early computer science, classical scientific inference,

04:04.840 --> 04:11.340
finding an answer that simply satisfies all the constraints of a problem is incredibly difficult.

04:11.560 --> 04:11.800
Right.

04:11.800 --> 04:18.880
Think about an old-school physics equation or, you know, balancing a complex chemical reaction.

04:19.160 --> 04:22.540
You plug in the mass, you plug in the velocity, you account for gravity,

04:22.540 --> 04:28.960
and you are hunting for the one single number that balances the entire equation.

04:29.160 --> 04:29.300
Right.

04:29.600 --> 04:33.940
Because there is only one way to perfectly balance a specific chemical equation.

04:33.940 --> 04:44.360
Or, like, there is only one correct orbital trajectory to send a probe to a specific crater on Mars based on the launch window and the fuel constraints.

04:44.440 --> 04:45.040
Exactly.

04:45.580 --> 04:53.000
The constraints in those systems are so tight, they are so restrictive, that correctness defines the outcome entirely.

04:53.000 --> 04:58.480
If you manage to find the correct answer, you have found the only possible answer.

04:59.220 --> 05:08.420
The system is heavily constrained, and your main job, like the computational heavy lifting, is just selecting that one needle in the haystack.

05:08.620 --> 05:08.880
Yeah.

05:09.120 --> 05:11.600
The difficulty is the search itself.

05:11.600 --> 05:23.200
But the paper argues that this classical assumption breaks down completely when we enter what modern learning theory calls the over-parameterized regime.

05:23.400 --> 05:23.880
Yes.

05:24.220 --> 05:26.080
And this is where things get really weird.

05:26.540 --> 05:32.660
When we talk about modern artificial intelligence, we aren't talking about a few variables in a physics equation.

05:32.660 --> 05:38.160
We're talking about massive models with billions, sometimes trillions of parameters.

05:38.160 --> 05:45.760
Yeah, and to really understand what a parameter is in this context, imagine a giant soundboard in a recording studio.

05:45.940 --> 05:46.100
Okay.

05:46.420 --> 05:49.120
A classical equation might have three or four knobs.

05:49.220 --> 05:51.660
You have to tune them perfectly to get a clear sound.

05:51.840 --> 05:55.240
But a modern neural network has a trillion knobs.

05:55.500 --> 05:56.540
That's a big soundboard.

05:56.880 --> 05:57.780
It's massive.

05:58.220 --> 06:06.760
And when you train these models on a data set, they don't just find one mathematical configuration that correctly classifies the data.

06:06.760 --> 06:19.100
Because they have so much excess capacity, like so many extra knobs to turn, they find a vast multiplicity of solutions that fit the training data exactly.

06:19.120 --> 06:20.140
Roughly, perfectly.

06:20.400 --> 06:20.700
Right.

06:21.180 --> 06:26.880
They achieve what is called zero empirical error in countless different ways.

06:26.960 --> 06:29.020
So it's not a needle in a haystack anymore.

06:29.240 --> 06:31.680
The whole haystack is made of needles.

06:31.980 --> 06:33.380
That is a great way to put it.

06:33.380 --> 06:35.380
The condition of correctness becomes degenerate.

06:35.380 --> 06:36.060
Degenerate.

06:36.280 --> 06:36.800
Degenerate.

06:36.880 --> 06:37.060
Yeah.

06:37.360 --> 06:50.500
The paper uses this term degeneracy, which in mathematics and physics just means you have multiple distinct states or configurations that all correspond to the exact same macroscopic constraints.

06:50.680 --> 06:50.940
Okay.

06:51.120 --> 06:53.320
They all look perfectly correct from the outside.

06:53.320 --> 07:03.120
So the AI perfectly identifies the picture of the cat, but the internal arrangement of those trillion knobs could be entirely different every time.

07:03.440 --> 07:03.560
Right.

07:03.620 --> 07:06.860
This creates what the author calls an admissible set.

07:07.220 --> 07:12.720
It is an entire high dimensional surface where millions of different solutions coexist.

07:12.720 --> 07:18.680
And every single one of them is technically flawless according to the rules of the game.

07:18.840 --> 07:21.740
Which brings us back to the GPS analogy from the start.

07:21.920 --> 07:22.100
Yeah.

07:22.560 --> 07:26.260
I have 10 million perfectly equal routes.

07:27.100 --> 07:30.840
Correctness is totally useless here as a filtering mechanism.

07:30.900 --> 07:31.440
Exactly.

07:31.440 --> 07:39.540
So if an AI is presented with an infinite surface of correct answers, my first instinct is that it just picks one at random.

07:39.540 --> 07:47.900
It just wanders aimlessly through that massive set of correct answers and stops wherever it happens to run out of computing time.

07:48.040 --> 07:50.360
Like it just rolls a billion sided die.

07:50.720 --> 07:51.420
You would think so.

07:51.500 --> 07:51.720
Right.

07:52.100 --> 07:56.900
But empirical results in deep learning show that is not what happens at all.

07:57.100 --> 07:57.600
Wait, really?

07:57.600 --> 07:58.100
Yeah.

07:58.620 --> 08:05.720
Modern machine learning systems consistently converge on very specific structured regions of solutions.

08:06.220 --> 08:08.560
They don't just pick any correct answer.

08:08.980 --> 08:12.440
They reliably pick certain types of correct answers.

08:12.740 --> 08:16.520
Which proves that correctness is not driving the car anymore.

08:17.140 --> 08:21.460
There is a hidden implicit bias doing the selecting.

08:21.460 --> 08:31.500
We have transitioned from a selection limited regime where finding any answer is hard to a constraint limited regime.

08:31.760 --> 08:33.060
Where answers are abundant.

08:33.340 --> 08:33.900
Exactly.

08:34.220 --> 08:38.840
Answers are incredibly abundant and choosing among them is the real challenge.

08:39.240 --> 08:43.060
And this is incredibly relevant to you, the listener, right now.

08:43.060 --> 08:58.620
Because whenever you are faced with a complex, high stakes decision in your own life where multiple choices technically solve the problem, your brain is doing exactly what these physics and AI models are doing.

08:58.800 --> 08:59.240
Yes.

08:59.240 --> 09:04.760
Let's say you were looking to buy a house and after months of searching, you find three houses.

09:05.320 --> 09:09.740
They're all in the right neighborhood, they all have the right number of bedrooms, and they are all exactly the same price.

09:09.940 --> 09:10.140
Yeah.

09:10.260 --> 09:13.160
They all perfectly satisfy your local constraints.

09:13.360 --> 09:15.320
They have zero empirical error.

09:15.520 --> 09:22.120
And when correctness can't break the tie, you subconsciously search for a secondary principle to make the selection.

09:22.120 --> 09:25.560
You don't just flip a coin for a 30-year mortgage.

09:26.000 --> 09:34.020
You are navigating an admissible set of your own, and you are looking for a hidden variable to resolve the degeneracy.

09:34.280 --> 09:36.100
So what is that hidden variable?

09:36.280 --> 09:41.820
I mean, if correctness isn't making the choice, we have to look for the invisible hand that is.

09:42.120 --> 09:49.520
And the paper formalizes this invisible tiebreaker mathematically as the bias functional.

09:49.520 --> 09:53.480
Right, the bias functional, denoted in the text as B of H.

09:53.600 --> 09:56.680
It is really the cornerstone of this entire framework.

09:56.840 --> 09:57.000
Right.

09:57.360 --> 10:01.180
In mathematics, a functional is essentially a function of a function.

10:01.640 --> 10:14.920
It takes an entire configuration, like an entire proposed reality, maybe one of those specific AI knob arrangements or one of those GPS routes, and assigns a single overarching number to it.

10:14.940 --> 10:15.880
Okay, so it scores it.

10:15.880 --> 10:26.740
Basically, in this theory, the bias functional assigns a measure of residual tension to every single configuration inside that massive set of correct answers.

10:26.920 --> 10:27.900
Residual tension.

10:28.200 --> 10:28.340
Yeah.

10:28.340 --> 10:38.020
So to translate that into our GPS scenario, among all those 10 million perfectly correct routes, some of them are tense and some of them are relaxed.

10:38.020 --> 10:51.840
They all get you to the store in 14 minutes, but maybe route A requires making 50 sharp, jarring turns through narrow back alleys, crossing three busy intersections without a stoplight.

10:52.340 --> 10:56.160
It technically works, but it's incredibly tense.

10:56.640 --> 11:02.760
Whereas route B is just a smooth, straight line down a quiet boulevard.

11:02.760 --> 11:03.760
Exactly.

11:04.260 --> 11:16.520
The core idea is that the system, whether it is a neural network, a physical field, or you driving your car, will automatically, inevitably seek to minimize this tension.

11:16.740 --> 11:18.100
It prefers the straight line.

11:18.200 --> 11:23.580
It prefers the straight line, even if both technically solve the problem in the exact same amount of time.

11:23.580 --> 11:29.440
And this concept of degeneracy resolution is not just an abstract philosophical idea.

11:29.680 --> 11:32.620
The paper argues it is an empirical necessity.

11:33.260 --> 11:34.160
Why is it a necessity?

11:34.280 --> 11:39.160
I mean, why can't a system just exist in a state of high tension if it's technically correct?

11:39.280 --> 11:40.200
What is stopping it?

11:40.360 --> 11:51.320
Because without this bias functional, without this natural drive to minimize residual tension, there is no principled explanation for why one reality emerges over another.

11:51.480 --> 11:52.080
Oh, I see.

11:52.080 --> 11:58.360
If everything is equally correct, why does this specific physical state manifest in the universe?

11:59.140 --> 12:09.280
Why does this specific AI model generalize well to new data, while another equally correct model fails spectacularly?

12:09.640 --> 12:12.860
The functional is what resolves the degeneracy.

12:12.860 --> 12:21.860
It takes a state of infinite equal possibilities and forces a physical selection by ranking them based on tension.

12:22.080 --> 12:23.960
Here's where it gets really interesting, though.

12:24.580 --> 12:29.260
The paper isn't just saying this is a clever trick programmers use to make your AI work better.

12:29.540 --> 12:33.740
It claims this functional is a fundamental law of nature.

12:33.740 --> 12:35.140
Yes, exactly.

12:35.380 --> 12:40.680
It applies to physical systems, like how microscopic particles settle into macroscopic states.

12:40.900 --> 12:44.600
It encodes the underlying preference structure of reality itself.

12:45.100 --> 12:47.520
But that raises a massive question for me.

12:47.640 --> 12:48.220
What's that?

12:48.360 --> 12:54.080
If systems desperately want to minimize this tension, where does that tension come from in the first place?

12:54.080 --> 12:57.080
If an answer is correct, why would it be tense?

12:57.480 --> 13:01.960
Ah, well that brings us to one of the most compelling mathematical concepts in the paper.

13:02.420 --> 13:07.120
The profound distinction between local success and global obstruction.

13:07.380 --> 13:08.580
Local versus global.

13:08.940 --> 13:09.240
Right.

13:09.700 --> 13:13.380
Tension doesn't usually come from a single broken piece.

13:13.980 --> 13:18.360
The tension comes from parts of a system that look completely correct in isolation,

13:18.360 --> 13:23.300
but clash violently when you try to assemble them into a larger whole.

13:23.760 --> 13:27.840
The paper uses the terms local admissibility versus global consistency.

13:28.120 --> 13:28.560
Exactly.

13:29.160 --> 13:34.200
And to explain this, the author introduces concepts from sheaf theory.

13:34.500 --> 13:35.140
Sheaf theory.

13:35.360 --> 13:35.540
Yeah.

13:35.540 --> 13:44.120
It is a branch of mathematics that deals specifically with how local data pieces together to form global structures.

13:44.120 --> 13:49.000
Think of it as the mathematics of sewing patches together to make a quilt.

13:49.740 --> 13:52.440
The author introduces the concept of an obstruction.

13:53.320 --> 13:59.420
Imagine a configuration that satisfies all the rules and constraints in its own little local bubble.

13:59.760 --> 14:01.400
We call this local admissibility.

14:01.960 --> 14:04.140
The patch is perfectly woven.

14:04.440 --> 14:04.560
Right.

14:04.680 --> 14:05.660
The patch looks great.

14:05.660 --> 14:13.100
But when you try to sew that local patch with all the other local patches to create a complete global quilt,

14:13.300 --> 14:14.920
it fails completely.

14:15.300 --> 14:16.660
The patterns don't line up.

14:16.960 --> 14:17.960
The edges pucker.

14:18.240 --> 14:23.100
And the paper says this happens because it violates the co-cycle condition on overlaps,

14:23.740 --> 14:28.300
which I have to admit sounds like something out of a graduate-level topology textbook.

14:28.680 --> 14:30.420
The terminology is dense.

14:30.540 --> 14:31.200
I will give you that.

14:31.200 --> 14:35.040
But the underlying mechanism is actually very intuitive.

14:35.960 --> 14:46.000
The co-cycle condition is just a strict mathematical requirement that local pieces of data must be perfectly compatible wherever their boundaries meet.

14:46.180 --> 14:47.200
Okay, so they have to match up.

14:47.480 --> 14:47.800
Yes.

14:48.280 --> 14:53.260
If patch A overlaps with patch B, they have to agree on the overlap.

14:53.580 --> 14:55.440
If they don't agree, you have an obstruction.

14:55.440 --> 15:01.380
You cannot build a global section, like a global reality, out of them.

15:01.460 --> 15:02.960
It's like writing a time travel movie.

15:03.480 --> 15:04.380
Oh, I like this.

15:04.500 --> 15:04.680
Yeah.

15:04.820 --> 15:05.680
Think about the script.

15:05.980 --> 15:06.140
Yeah.

15:06.400 --> 15:08.580
Scene one is set in the year 2024.

15:09.360 --> 15:11.420
The main character builds a time machine.

15:12.060 --> 15:14.240
The logic within that scene is tight.

15:14.500 --> 15:15.760
The dialogue is great.

15:16.140 --> 15:19.380
The physics within that specific room make total sense.

15:19.700 --> 15:21.040
It is locally admissible.

15:21.240 --> 15:21.500
Right.

15:21.780 --> 15:24.120
Scene two is set in the year 1990.

15:24.120 --> 15:27.560
The character's father finds a weird device.

15:27.900 --> 15:28.020
Yeah.

15:28.280 --> 15:30.640
That scene also works perfectly in isolation.

15:31.000 --> 15:39.880
But when you put scene one and scene two together and try to make a cohesive two-hour movie, suddenly the timeline makes absolutely no sense.

15:40.220 --> 15:46.240
The character is meeting their past self before their past self even built the machine.

15:46.700 --> 15:49.260
The movie fails the cozy coastal condition.

15:49.920 --> 15:52.440
There's a massive global obstruction.

15:52.440 --> 15:58.040
That analogy captures the exact mathematical dilemma perfectly.

15:59.240 --> 16:01.120
The scenes are locally correct.

16:01.280 --> 16:04.000
They have zero empirical error in their own little bubbles.

16:04.400 --> 16:10.220
But they create immense residual tension when forced into a global structure.

16:10.360 --> 16:11.660
Because they contradict each other.

16:11.660 --> 16:12.140
Right.

16:12.140 --> 16:20.640
And if we connect this to the bigger picture of the paper, the goal of the selection process isn't just passively hunting for a state of low tension.

16:20.960 --> 16:28.640
It is actively hunting down and eliminating configurations that cannot be extended to global coherence.

16:28.640 --> 16:33.520
It is searching for those timeline plot holes and eradicating them.

16:33.520 --> 16:42.220
So realization, like the act of a possibility actually crossing the threshold into becoming a real tangible fact, it requires both things.

16:42.340 --> 16:46.040
It requires admissibility, meaning it follows the local rules of the scene.

16:46.180 --> 16:48.880
And it requires the complete absence of obstruction.

16:49.120 --> 16:49.480
Yes.

16:49.560 --> 16:53.080
Meaning it glues together perfectly across all scenes, across all scales.

16:53.080 --> 16:55.880
Reality is the movie with no plot holes.

16:56.420 --> 17:00.280
Which brings us to how we express that exact moment mathematically.

17:01.940 --> 17:08.960
How do we pinpoint the moment a locally admissible possibility becomes a realized, globally coherent fact?

17:09.640 --> 17:16.080
The paper introduces a mechanism called the realization operator, denoted as a capital R.

17:16.560 --> 17:18.060
The realization operator.

17:18.140 --> 17:19.060
Let's break this down.

17:19.600 --> 17:22.380
What is this operator actually doing in the math?

17:22.380 --> 17:25.060
Think of the operator R as a filter.

17:26.160 --> 17:35.800
It takes that massive overwhelming set of all possible correct configurations, the whole admissible set we talked about, and systematically reduces it.

17:35.920 --> 17:37.160
It filters out the tension.

17:37.680 --> 17:37.860
Oh, yeah.

17:37.960 --> 17:44.860
When the realization operator acts on a configuration, it maps it to the state of absolute minimum residual tension.

17:45.620 --> 17:49.140
It is the mathematical expression of reality solidifying.

17:49.140 --> 17:56.780
But I noticed a specific mathematical property the author highlights here, proposition 4.1, the idempotence of realization.

17:57.440 --> 18:00.260
The equation is R of R of H equals R of H.

18:00.360 --> 18:01.560
Yes, idempotence.

18:01.660 --> 18:03.080
I stared at that for a while.

18:03.260 --> 18:03.440
What?

18:03.500 --> 18:06.940
What does it actually mean for reality to be idempotent?

18:06.940 --> 18:14.720
So, in mathematics, an operation is idempotent if applying it multiple times has the exact same effect as applying it just once.

18:14.860 --> 18:15.280
Like what?

18:15.500 --> 18:18.880
A simple example is the absolute value of a number.

18:19.280 --> 18:21.840
The absolute value of negative 5 is 5.

18:22.580 --> 18:26.460
If you take the absolute value of that result again, it's still 5.

18:26.460 --> 18:31.260
You can apply the operation a billion times, and the answer never changes.

18:31.780 --> 18:33.560
Or think of a sorting algorithm.

18:34.100 --> 18:43.280
If you sort a list of numbers from smallest to largest, and then you run the exact same sorting algorithm on that list again, nothing moves.

18:43.440 --> 18:44.600
The list is already sorted.

18:44.880 --> 18:45.540
Ah, I see.

18:45.540 --> 18:57.760
So, once a configuration has been fully realized, once it hits that absolute minimum tension and becomes globally coherent, applying the selection process to it again doesn't change anything.

18:57.900 --> 18:58.380
Exactly.

18:58.620 --> 19:00.380
It has become a fixed point.

19:00.720 --> 19:03.680
It has survived the decimation of all other alternatives.

19:04.360 --> 19:06.780
There are no more timeline plot holes to fix.

19:06.920 --> 19:10.120
No more local overlaps that violate the co-cycle condition.

19:10.120 --> 19:18.160
If the universe runs its reality-checking algorithm over that configuration a second time, it just returns the exact same state.

19:18.460 --> 19:22.060
R of R of H is just R of H. It is ultimate stability.

19:22.440 --> 19:24.020
But let's be realistic here.

19:24.560 --> 19:31.040
The universe, or an AI, doesn't just snap its fingers and instantly calculate this fixed point.

19:31.720 --> 19:32.820
It's not magic.

19:33.580 --> 19:36.320
An AI takes days or weeks to train.

19:36.320 --> 19:41.060
The universe took billions of years to cool into its current physical laws.

19:41.740 --> 19:44.360
The paper is very careful to clarify this.

19:44.760 --> 19:49.320
The realization operator R is an abstract mathematical limit.

19:49.840 --> 19:50.820
It is the destination.

19:51.560 --> 19:51.680
Okay.

19:51.880 --> 19:57.540
In the real world, systems evolve toward that destination through a dynamic process.

19:58.260 --> 20:02.840
The paper uses the Greek letter phi to represent this update rule.

20:02.840 --> 20:06.340
A system iteratively evolves over time.

20:06.780 --> 20:07.180
Iteratively.

20:07.540 --> 20:07.840
Yes.

20:08.160 --> 20:14.820
It checks a configuration, finds some tension, reduces it slightly while making sure it still obeys the local rules, and repeats.

20:15.760 --> 20:18.880
Reality isn't a sudden, omniscient calculation.

20:19.280 --> 20:21.820
Reality is the limit of a dynamical evolution.

20:22.420 --> 20:27.540
It is where the system eventually settles after exhausting all its tension-reducing iterations.

20:28.060 --> 20:28.340
Okay.

20:28.440 --> 20:30.200
So we've been talking about this tension a lot.

20:30.200 --> 20:37.880
We've talked about it abstractly as the thing the universe wants to get rid of, the plot holes in the movie, the sharp turns on the GPS route.

20:38.360 --> 20:39.580
What exactly is it?

20:39.740 --> 20:39.820
Right.

20:39.940 --> 20:41.840
That is the million-dollar question.

20:41.960 --> 20:50.980
Because if this theory truly unifies everything from black holes to the stock market, tension has to mean something specific in different fields.

20:50.980 --> 20:55.720
And this is where the paper makes its boldest, most sweeping claim.

20:56.340 --> 21:02.500
It unites three entirely different fields of science by defining the three phases of tension.

21:02.500 --> 21:08.600
This is the bias equivalence principle, outlined as principle 5.1 in the text.

21:09.140 --> 21:13.580
The author argues that residual tension isn't just one monolithic thing.

21:13.980 --> 21:21.120
It has three specific coordinate representations, depending entirely on what kind of system you're observing.

21:21.120 --> 21:21.480
Right.

21:21.840 --> 21:27.680
Let's walk through these three phases, because this is where the paper really tries to pull the grand unified theory together.

21:28.500 --> 21:36.000
First, in geometric systems, so think physical space, gravity, the shape of the universe, tension is measured as curvature.

21:36.240 --> 21:37.180
Curvature, exactly.

21:37.380 --> 21:43.560
The paper says high second derivatives mean sharp variations, and sharp variations are inherently unstable.

21:43.560 --> 21:47.400
The system desperately wants geometric smoothness.

21:47.780 --> 21:52.960
If you have a crumpled piece of paper, the physical tension is in the sharp creases.

21:53.380 --> 21:55.120
It naturally wants to flatten out.

21:55.520 --> 22:03.620
Second, in thermodynamic or informational systems, tension is measured as unresolved entropy, or ambiguity.

22:04.040 --> 22:04.720
Ambiguity.

22:04.900 --> 22:05.200
Yes.

22:05.620 --> 22:09.940
A system that lacks informational closure has a high degree of degeneracy.

22:09.940 --> 22:16.640
Think of a blurry image, or a gas trapped in a box where you don't know the exact position of the molecules.

22:17.180 --> 22:22.620
The system naturally wants to reduce uncertainty and promote strict determinacy.

22:22.960 --> 22:24.740
It wants to resolve the information.

22:25.320 --> 22:33.340
And third, and this is the one that really made me pause, in economic systems, tension is measured as future work.

22:33.580 --> 22:34.360
Future work.

22:34.360 --> 22:38.720
The cost of future corrections, repairs, or stabilizing interventions.

22:38.720 --> 22:42.760
An economic system wants temporal stability.

22:43.120 --> 22:43.240
Right.

22:43.480 --> 22:54.060
If a company sets up a supply chain that requires constant, exhausting, daily micromanagement just to keep products moving, that system has massive residual tension.

22:54.460 --> 22:57.580
The future work required to maintain it is immense.

22:57.920 --> 22:58.900
But, hold on.

22:58.980 --> 23:00.800
I'm trying to wrap my head around this.

23:01.240 --> 23:05.580
And I imagine you, listening right now, might be doing the same.

23:05.580 --> 23:25.540
It sounds wild, I know.

23:25.540 --> 23:28.280
It sounds like a poetic metaphor, not hard mathematics.

23:28.840 --> 23:33.680
How does the paper justify linking these completely disparate concepts?

23:34.140 --> 23:37.980
It is a staggering claim, but the justification is rigorous.

23:38.720 --> 23:46.280
The bias equivalence principle isn't saying that curvature, entropy, and future work are the exact same number.

23:47.040 --> 23:52.580
You can't just swap the units of a geometric angle for the units of a dollar or joules per kelvin.

23:52.640 --> 23:53.740
Right, that would be absurd.

23:53.740 --> 23:59.860
What the principle states is that they induce equivalent orderings on the space of admissible configurations.

24:00.300 --> 24:02.100
Equivalent orderings, meaning what?

24:02.740 --> 24:10.140
If I have a list of ten possible configurations, all three of these measurements will rank them in the exact same order from worst to best.

24:10.580 --> 24:11.220
Precisely.

24:11.620 --> 24:18.960
They all measure the exact same underlying structural quality, distance from a stable fixed point.

24:19.580 --> 24:21.220
Think about the mechanics of it.

24:21.220 --> 24:25.160
Curvature is instability under the rules of geometry.

24:25.840 --> 24:32.400
If you have a sharp bend in a physical field, geometric forces will automatically act to smooth it out.

24:32.600 --> 24:33.560
Okay, I tracked that.

24:33.560 --> 25:02.780
So in every single case, regardless of the field, a higher value means the configuration simply cannot persist without correction.

25:02.780 --> 25:04.680
It has a high cost of existence.

25:04.900 --> 25:05.340
Exactly.

25:05.340 --> 25:16.360
The paper argues that what varies across these domains is not the fundamental law of selection, but simply the language, the units, in which the cost of that selection is measured.

25:16.780 --> 25:20.720
They are different shadows cast by the exact same mathematical object.

25:20.720 --> 25:22.720
That is mind-blowing.

25:22.720 --> 25:41.200
It means whether a star system is forming from a chaotic cloud of gas, or a neural network is learning to recognize faces, or a global society is structuring its trade routes, they are all mathematically running the exact same optimization algorithm.

25:41.540 --> 25:41.940
Yes.

25:41.940 --> 25:50.280
They are all just trying to slide down the hill to the lowest state of tension, which brings us perfectly to the actual mechanics.

25:50.800 --> 25:54.240
How do these systems actually get rid of this tension?

25:54.940 --> 25:57.840
Because it's one thing to say they want to deduce tension.

25:58.000 --> 26:04.880
It's another to explain how a trillion-parameter AI or physical field physically does it.

26:04.880 --> 26:05.160
Right.

26:05.160 --> 26:10.720
The paper outlines two main ways this happens, a continuous process and a discrete process.

26:11.160 --> 26:12.640
Let's start with the continuous one.

26:12.920 --> 26:23.380
So in continuous systems like classical physical fields or certain types of continuous AI training algorithms, the reduction of tension happens through what is called gradient flow.

26:23.600 --> 26:24.340
Gradient flow.

26:24.460 --> 26:26.020
That sounds incredibly dense.

26:26.020 --> 26:30.560
It sounds intimidating, but the concept is very grounded.

26:31.380 --> 26:36.020
The system calculates the variational derivative of the bias functional.

26:36.740 --> 26:42.920
In plain terms, it looks at the entire high-dimensional landscape of tension around its current state.

26:43.320 --> 26:46.340
It maps out all the immediate possibilities.

26:46.340 --> 26:56.380
Then it finds the steepest possible slope downwards, the path that will reduce the tension the fastest, and takes a step in that direction.

26:56.900 --> 26:58.360
Let me try an analogy here.

26:58.600 --> 27:00.760
It's like water flowing down a mountain.

27:01.100 --> 27:01.500
Perfect.

27:02.000 --> 27:04.920
The water absolutely has to stay on the surface of the mountain.

27:05.280 --> 27:07.980
That represents our constraint, the local rules.

27:08.080 --> 27:08.840
It can't break.

27:09.200 --> 27:12.540
The water can't just float into the sky or phase through the rock.

27:12.540 --> 27:22.680
But while staying on the mountain, gravity will always force the water to find the steepest, most direct path down to the lowest possible valley.

27:23.340 --> 27:28.540
It minimizes its gravitational tension by flowing continuously along the gradient.

27:28.860 --> 27:31.380
That is exactly how gradient flow works.

27:31.700 --> 27:37.620
The paper mathematically notes that this flow is projected onto the tangent space of the admissible set.

27:37.920 --> 27:39.660
Projected onto the tangent space.

27:39.660 --> 27:44.500
Yeah, that phrase is exactly your water staying on the surface of the mountain.

27:45.240 --> 27:56.140
The system evolves continuously, dissipating energy, smoothing out irregularities, and reducing future work, all while strictly adhering to the constraints.

27:56.960 --> 28:00.460
But not all systems in the universe are smooth and continuous.

28:01.600 --> 28:06.240
Water flows, but human reasoning doesn't flow like water.

28:06.240 --> 28:09.800
Water, computer code doesn't flow like water.

28:10.080 --> 28:13.020
This is where the paper introduces the discrete alternative.

28:14.100 --> 28:14.820
Decimation.

28:15.200 --> 28:24.480
If gradient flow is like water smoothly flowing down a hill, decimation is like a sculptor aggressively chiseling away chunks of marble.

28:24.940 --> 28:25.580
Decimation.

28:26.040 --> 28:26.920
It's a strong word.

28:27.260 --> 28:29.680
What is the system actually doing when it decimates?

28:30.100 --> 28:34.600
Decimation is the process of identifying specific incoherent modes.

28:34.600 --> 28:44.900
Parts of a system whose contribution to the overall tension is simply too high, parts that violate the cosecle condition we talked about earlier, and it completely removes them.

28:44.980 --> 28:45.800
It just leaks them.

28:45.940 --> 28:48.220
You don't smooth them out, you excise them.

28:48.580 --> 28:55.800
You do this iteratively, chopping away the unstable parts until only a stable core, a coherent configuration, is left standing.

28:55.800 --> 29:05.800
And the paper explicitly links this discrete chiseling process to how AI reasoning works, referencing something called the CLIO framework.

29:07.280 --> 29:11.820
CLIO stands for Cognitive Loop Via In-Situ Optimization.

29:12.500 --> 29:20.200
It breaks down discrete reasoning into a repetitive loop of three steps, exploration, evaluation, and pruning.

29:20.200 --> 29:30.000
Yes. When a discrete reasoning system, like an advanced language model, tries to solve a complex logic puzzle, it generates a proposed thought or trajectory.

29:30.280 --> 29:31.160
That's exploration.

29:31.660 --> 29:36.320
It then evaluates how much uncertainty or tension that trajectory holds.

29:36.680 --> 29:42.560
If the tension is too high, if it senses a global obstruction down the line, it prunes it.

29:42.900 --> 29:46.100
It cuts that branch of logic off completely and tries another.

29:46.100 --> 29:50.720
This raises a truly fascinating diagnostic detail hidden in the text.

29:51.420 --> 29:58.520
How can you tell from the outside if a system is successfully reasoning its way to reality, or if it is stuck?

29:58.660 --> 30:03.280
The paper notes a brilliant empirical observation from recursive reasoning systems.

30:03.940 --> 30:12.600
Correct reasoning trajectories, the ones that are actually heading toward a globally consistent reality, show a monotonic reduction in uncertainty.

30:12.600 --> 30:18.300
Meaning the uncertainty goes down steadily, step by step, never increasing.

30:18.620 --> 30:20.300
It's a smooth ride down the mountain.

30:20.580 --> 30:20.840
Yes.

30:21.420 --> 30:27.600
But incorrect trajectories, the ones harboring a hidden flaw, they show persistent oscillation.

30:28.400 --> 30:31.040
Persistent oscillation. They bounce back and forth.

30:31.360 --> 30:35.960
The system physically cannot settle if there is unresolved tension.

30:36.480 --> 30:38.400
Think back to our time travel movie.

30:38.400 --> 30:50.840
If there is a hidden global obstruction, a massive plot hole that hasn't been fixed, the system's uncertainty will drop as it writes a scene, then suddenly spike again as it hits the contradiction.

30:51.060 --> 30:51.540
Oh, I see.

30:51.620 --> 30:56.820
Then it dropped as it tries a quick workaround, then spikes again because the workaround created a new paradox.

30:57.560 --> 30:58.760
It oscillates.

30:58.760 --> 31:01.620
The paper states this as a fundamental rule.

31:02.140 --> 31:08.540
Under a constraint-closure dynamic, unresolved inconsistency must manifest as persistent uncertainty.

31:09.280 --> 31:16.280
Convergence, or reaching true reality, necessarily produces a smooth, monotonic decay of tension.

31:16.860 --> 31:23.540
So if a system is vibrating, if it is oscillating back and forth in its uncertainty, it is carrying a global obstruction.

31:23.540 --> 31:27.000
It has high residual tension and will eventually be decimated.

31:27.300 --> 31:28.740
I love that diagnostic.

31:29.020 --> 31:29.960
It is very powerful.

31:30.520 --> 31:44.180
And speaking of persistent oscillation and systems carrying high residual tension, let's bring this grand theoretical framework directly out of the realm of physics and AI and right into the listener's daily life.

31:44.340 --> 31:46.420
Let's apply this to the modern economy.

31:46.740 --> 31:47.960
Yes, let's do that.

31:47.960 --> 31:49.600
Because the paper actually does this.

31:49.820 --> 31:50.040
Yeah.

31:50.180 --> 31:55.320
And it is arguably the most tangible, eye-opening part of the entire deep dive.

31:55.320 --> 31:58.480
It is where the abstract mathematics hit the pavement.

31:59.100 --> 32:08.280
If we are going to treat the economy as one of these complex, over-parameterized systems, we first have to define its admissible set.

32:08.740 --> 32:12.160
In physics, the constraints are gravity and the speed of light.

32:12.160 --> 32:21.460
In economics, the constraints are things like budget balance, resource availability, contractual obligations, and local market feasibility.

32:21.900 --> 32:23.420
And what is the bias functional?

32:23.680 --> 32:26.820
What is the tension the economy is constantly trying to minimize?

32:27.240 --> 32:27.440
Yeah.

32:27.900 --> 32:34.240
As we covered earlier with the three phases of tension, in economics, residual tension is future work.

32:34.360 --> 32:34.860
Exactly.

32:34.860 --> 32:43.660
It is the effort, the capital, the sheer human exhaustion required to maintain a specific structural configuration over time.

32:43.900 --> 32:51.720
To illustrate this, the paper dives into a highly relevant case study, attention-based advertising systems.

32:52.320 --> 32:57.760
Think about the social media engagement loops we all interact with on a daily basis.

32:57.760 --> 33:00.600
The paper doesn't look at these psychologically.

33:01.300 --> 33:09.220
It looks at them purely as structural configurations, analyzing them mathematically through the lens of residual tension.

33:09.220 --> 33:11.180
And let's be very clear here.

33:11.460 --> 33:17.280
We aren't taking a political or ideological stance on big tech companies, and neither is the paper.

33:17.560 --> 33:22.640
We are simply applying the mathematical and structural analysis provided in the text.

33:22.640 --> 33:29.780
And what the paper points out is that these social media engagement loops perfectly satisfy all the local constraints.

33:30.420 --> 33:32.460
Locally, they are highly admissible.

33:33.020 --> 33:35.260
Think about a single interaction on an app.

33:35.520 --> 33:43.780
A user logs on, they are shown a piece of targeted content, they click it, and a fraction of a cent of revenue is generated for the platform.

33:44.000 --> 33:45.040
Right, local success.

33:45.440 --> 33:48.340
The metrics look fantastic in that isolated moment.

33:48.340 --> 33:54.400
The local rules of the economy, exchanging value for attention, are completely satisfied.

33:55.240 --> 33:59.620
There is zero empirical error in that specific transaction.

34:00.640 --> 34:11.480
However, the paper argues that these specific configurations, when viewed globally over time, encode massive, unsustainable levels of future work.

34:11.760 --> 34:13.520
Where is the hidden tension?

34:13.520 --> 34:18.860
Why does a simple engagement loop require endless future work?

34:18.860 --> 34:26.200
Well, to sustain that endless loop of engagement, the system requires continuous escalating recalibration.

34:27.120 --> 34:28.880
Think about user fatigue.

34:29.480 --> 34:33.060
A human being naturally habituates to stimuli.

34:33.800 --> 34:36.540
You eventually get bored of the same type of content.

34:36.640 --> 34:37.220
Yeah, of course.

34:37.220 --> 34:39.120
So the algorithm has to work harder.

34:39.640 --> 34:41.620
It has to increase the informational load.

34:41.700 --> 34:51.980
It has to deploy escalating interventions, more sensational content, more precise behavioral targeting, faster context switching, just to counteract your natural saturation.

34:52.680 --> 34:58.960
The system must expend a constantly growing amount of effort just to maintain its current level of coherence.

34:59.080 --> 35:01.400
It's the persistent oscillation we just talked about.

35:01.400 --> 35:11.400
The system is constantly vibrating, constantly tweaking and shocking the user with a new notification or a wilder video, just to keep the local numbers from dropping.

35:12.120 --> 35:15.200
So what does this all mean for the broader economy?

35:16.060 --> 35:18.560
How does the framework judge these systems?

35:19.280 --> 35:22.900
It demands a complete redefinition of economic efficiency.

35:23.700 --> 35:27.840
Traditionally, we define efficiency as static optimality.

35:27.840 --> 35:30.840
Are we maximizing profit right this second?

35:31.260 --> 35:32.740
Is the quarterly report green?

35:33.500 --> 35:37.800
But the paper argues that true efficiency is dynamic stability.

35:38.920 --> 35:47.240
A configuration is only truly efficient, only truly real, if it minimizes the future work required to maintain itself over time.

35:47.560 --> 35:52.580
The paper explicitly calls out proxy-driven, speculative systems.

35:52.580 --> 35:59.560
Systems that rely entirely on capturing attention or capital inflows without building any underlying structural stability.

35:59.820 --> 35:59.940
Right.

36:00.140 --> 36:02.240
They might look incredibly successful locally.

36:02.700 --> 36:03.960
The stock price might be high.

36:04.080 --> 36:06.380
The daily active user metrics might be off the charts.

36:06.620 --> 36:13.040
But the laws of reality, the exact same mathematical laws that govern the curvature of space-time and the entropy of a gas,

36:13.740 --> 36:17.080
dictate that these systems have dangerously high residual tension.

36:17.360 --> 36:20.940
And the mathematical prediction of the paper is stark.

36:20.940 --> 36:31.500
Because the universe inevitably seeks to minimize residual tension, configurations with escalating future work are dynamically unstable.

36:32.460 --> 36:41.480
Absent endless external life support, these high-tension attention loops will eventually collapse under their own accumulated corrective burden.

36:41.740 --> 36:42.080
Wow.

36:42.440 --> 36:45.040
They fail to achieve global coherence.

36:45.460 --> 36:48.340
In the language of the paper, they will be decimated.

36:48.340 --> 36:50.880
They will be eliminated by reality.

36:51.000 --> 36:52.340
Eliminated by reality.

36:52.780 --> 36:54.520
You can fake it locally for a while.

36:54.660 --> 36:56.300
You can pump up the local metrics.

36:56.760 --> 37:00.280
But the global co-cycle condition always comes due.

37:00.680 --> 37:03.360
You can't cheat the bias equivalence principle.

37:03.700 --> 37:04.240
You cannot.

37:04.640 --> 37:07.820
And that brings us beautifully to the conclusion of the paper's argument.

37:08.280 --> 37:10.320
We have traced this incredible journey.

37:10.320 --> 37:21.200
We've seen how reality forms from the algorithms training artificial intelligence, to the physical fields governing matter, to the social media feeds dominating our economy.

37:21.520 --> 37:24.880
Let's pull it all together into the final paradigm shift.

37:24.880 --> 37:28.220
Theorem 10.1, Constraint Closure Selection.

37:28.720 --> 37:30.960
This is the grand summary of the entire paper.

37:31.100 --> 37:31.940
Let's state it clearly.

37:32.260 --> 37:37.300
Theorem 10.1 formally defines reality as constraint closure.

37:38.120 --> 37:43.340
It states that reality is not merely a configuration that satisfies local rules.

37:44.120 --> 37:52.880
Reality is a configuration that remains consistent under all admissible extensions, across all scales, and minimizes residual tension.

37:52.880 --> 37:54.160
It's the fixed point.

37:54.260 --> 37:54.560
Yes.

37:55.080 --> 37:57.700
It is the fixed point of the realization operator.

37:57.980 --> 37:59.880
It is the state that survives decimation.

38:00.880 --> 38:11.620
And the ultimate philosophical takeaway here, the thing that completely flips our everyday assumption on its head, is the profound difference between construction and elimination.

38:12.340 --> 38:17.480
We started this deep dive talking about how we all assume reality is built piece by piece.

38:17.940 --> 38:21.960
We assume we are constructing the right answer, adding truth upon truth.

38:22.100 --> 38:22.620
Right.

38:22.880 --> 38:25.680
But Flyxion's paper argues the exact opposite.

38:26.400 --> 38:29.440
Reality is not built by accumulating what is possible.

38:30.120 --> 38:33.360
Reality is revealed by eliminating what cannot coexist.

38:33.940 --> 38:35.760
It is the residue of decimation.

38:35.760 --> 38:45.940
When you chip away every single localized truth that creates global friction, when you erase every timeline that causes a paradox, what you are left holding is reality.

38:46.200 --> 38:48.900
It is a subtractive process, not an additive one.

38:48.900 --> 38:51.940
It completely changes how you look at the world.

38:51.940 --> 39:04.820
You stop looking at a complex system, a soaring skyscraper, a thriving ecosystem, or a flawless AI, and asking, how was this built?

39:04.820 --> 39:13.100
You start asking, what massive amount of chaos had to be eliminated for this to be the only thing left standing?

39:13.100 --> 39:24.680
And that framework applies far beyond physics or algorithms, which leads to a thought that wasn't explicitly stated in the paper, but builds directly on everything we've unpacked today.

39:24.680 --> 39:33.480
If reality is simply the residue left behind after eliminating all states of high tension in future work, look at your own life.

39:33.680 --> 39:34.800
Oh, I like where this is going.

39:34.880 --> 39:36.620
Applying the functional to ourselves.

39:36.620 --> 39:41.300
Think about the times you are feeling persistent, oscillating uncertainty.

39:42.040 --> 39:53.640
Maybe it's exhaustion in a relationship, a career path that feels like it's constantly fighting you, or a project that requires escalating interventions just to keep it from falling apart entirely.

39:53.900 --> 39:55.080
Yeah, we all have those.

39:55.260 --> 39:59.180
We usually just call that being stressed or having a run of bad luck.

39:59.180 --> 40:05.080
But according to the fundamental laws of physics and information outlined in this paper, you aren't just stressed.

40:05.340 --> 40:08.220
You are existing in a state of high residual tension.

40:09.020 --> 40:10.620
You are locally admissible.

40:11.160 --> 40:12.880
Like, you are showing up to work.

40:13.140 --> 40:14.140
You are paying the bills.

40:14.300 --> 40:15.560
You are technically functioning.

40:16.260 --> 40:19.740
But you are globally obstructed.

40:20.180 --> 40:24.680
The pieces of your life are violating the co-cycle condition.

40:24.680 --> 40:33.900
You are in a configuration that the universe, by its very nature, is actively trying to eliminate through gradient flow or decimation.

40:34.060 --> 40:34.260
Yeah.

40:34.460 --> 40:40.600
The tension you feel is the physical manifestation of future work that cannot be sustained.

40:40.720 --> 40:40.920
Right.

40:40.920 --> 40:49.140
So the provocative question you have to ask yourself is, what incoherent modes in your life do you need to let the system prune?

40:49.140 --> 40:58.840
What local optimization, what short-term wins or comfortable habits are actually creating massive global obstructions down the line?

40:59.060 --> 41:06.420
Because until you let the decimation happen, until you start fighting the closure of constraints, you will never reach your stable fixed point.

41:06.840 --> 41:07.680
What a question to ponder.

41:07.800 --> 41:07.980
Yeah.

41:08.120 --> 41:12.500
What do you need to eliminate to finally reach your fixed point of reality?

41:12.500 --> 41:16.980
We started this conversation talking about how the simple hunt for correctness was broken.

41:17.660 --> 41:20.720
Just finding a right answer wasn't enough anymore.

41:20.980 --> 41:21.180
Yeah.

41:21.240 --> 41:22.240
And now we know why.

41:22.580 --> 41:24.640
Because it's not about finding the right answer.

41:25.260 --> 41:29.740
It's about surviving the elimination of all the wrong ones.

41:31.000 --> 41:34.800
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive into the selection of reality.

41:35.400 --> 41:38.140
Keep questioning the tension, and we'll catch you next time.
